WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.139 --> 00:00:06.139
Working within a game engine brings its own unique set of ways to approach it.

2
00:00:06.139 --> 00:00:09.140
So we're going to go over those right now.

3
00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:13.140
So animating within a game engine, you have different types of animations within it.

4
00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:17.140
So depending on how you wish to approach or how you wish to get that animation,

5
00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:20.140
we want to familiarize you with those.

6
00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:22.140
Now one is called an input animation.

7
00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:28.140
Now this is typically where you want to take a controller and to control the character.

8
00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:34.140
So this is typically more for your real time or for your gaming type of things or even a user input scenario.

9
00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:40.140
Automotives can use it to have a customer play around with certain things so they may have an iPad

10
00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:47.140
or some other form of input control that's going to control these characters or control the changes done via an input.

11
00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:51.140
So those animations are then controlled in real time.

12
00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:54.140
Now the next one is called the linear animation.

13
00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:59.140
Now you'll be bringing in your animation, and this is pretty typical for movie animations and so forth,

14
00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:06.620
where you're bringing in that file via an FBX or some other pre-recorded mocap animation typically.

15
00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:11.620
You can also bring in Olympic, which is for raw data frame by frame caches,

16
00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:14.620
and that's more for changes in the vertices.

17
00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:18.620
And so that would be more like your cloth effects and so forth like that would be brought in.

18
00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:20.620
Then you'd apply it into Sequencer.

19
00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:27.620
Now using a control rig, you can keyframe directly in Sequencer or even use animation and adapt it even further.

20
00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:29.620
Now the third one is Live Link.

21
00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:33.620
Now you may have heard of this. This is how you can use, which is a plug-in,

22
00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:39.620
either through a mocap suit, Maya, or some other 3D application, and you drive the animation live.

23
00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:41.620
So what you'd be capturing you would be seeing that.

24
00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:46.620
So you can then use Take Recorder to input the animation, record it directly within Sequencer,

25
00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:49.620
playing over the top, even layering your animations.

26
00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:53.620
So this is a very useful way of bringing in your animations.

27
00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:56.680
Now we want to discuss something else that's called root motion.

28
00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:59.680
So this is just yet another form of your input animation.

29
00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:02.680
So we have it as in-place or game-driven motion.

30
00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:05.680
Now these are great like it states for input.

31
00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:08.680
These animations are in place, but they don't go anywhere.

32
00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:15.680
So the Animation Blueprint will use this to push it around within the capsule so your root bone doesn't move.

33
00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:20.810
Now the problem you can see above is the root motion not activated or extracted into the capsule.

34
00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:26.810
You'll see the character on the right, he jumps outside of the capsule and then snaps back.

35
00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:30.900
Obviously, you would not want that for your typical animations.

36
00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:35.900
Now animation-driven that goes someplace or root motion is intended for Sequencer to drive it.

37
00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:40.900
So you are going to be moving that capsule, moving the root motion as you can see on the left.

38
00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:43.900
So that way it doesn't snap back into place.

39
00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:49.900
Now there is an enable root motion option that will force the capsule to move according to that root motion.

40
00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:54.900
Gets a little confusing, but as you work with it more and more, you will become more familiarized where it works.
